# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.3 has "ought_be_law_lingers"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "even in such cases the idea of the breach of what ought to be law still lingers in a modified shape" is false. Is S3, "But even here, the idea of the breach of what ought to be law, still lingers in a modified shape.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2 has "pleased_punish ∧ bound_means_compelled ∧ gratified_enforced → ought_be_law_lingers" and "ought_be_law_lingers → legal_constraint_generating"; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.2 has "ought_be_law_lingers → legal_constraint_generating"; they differ when only "it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts we deem unjust should be punished", "it is an ordinary form of language to say that a person bound in justice to do a thing ought to be compelled to do it" and "we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power" are true, which the second allows; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.3 has "ought_be_law_lingers ∧ pleased_punish ∧ bound_means_compelled ∧ gratified_enforced → legal_constraint_generating"
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts we deem unjust should be punished and it is an ordinary form of language to say that a person bound in justice to do a thing ought to be compelled to do it and we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power), then even in such cases the idea of the breach of what ought to be law still lingers in a modified shape"; mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.2 does not
mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-3.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it would always give us pleasure, and chime in with our feelings of fitness, that acts we deem unjust should be punished and it is an ordinary form of language to say that a person bound in justice to do a thing ought to be compelled to do it and we should be gratified to see the obligation enforced by anybody who had the power), then even in such cases the idea of the breach of what ought to be law still lingers in a modified shape"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
